cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or data, please cite it as below."
title: "Tool-Call Reliability Bench: repeated-trial reliability of small local LLM tool calls in English and Roman Urdu"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-11-15
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24339821"
type: software
abstract: "Tool-use evaluations usually score one attempt per task, but an agent that books the right appointment four times out of five is not safe to leave unattended. We measure pass^k, the probability that all k attempts at the same task are correct, for two small open-weight models run locally (llama3.1:8b and qwen2.5:3b) on eight physiotherapy-clinic tool calls requested in English and in Roman Urdu, the Latin-script Urdu that many people in Pakistan and its diaspora type. Each task was attempted five times in two ways: with five wordings of the same request at temperature 0, and with one wording sampled five times at temperature 0.7 (320 calls). In English, pass^5 was 87.5% for llama3.1:8b and 75.0% for qwen2.5:3b in both conditions. In Roman Urdu it fell to 12.5–37.5%, and single attempts hid the gap: with five wordings, qwen2.5:3b got 62.5% of single attempts right but only 12.5% of tasks right every time. Rewording made Roman Urdu tasks intermittently wrong; sampling made them systematically wrong, with up to half of the tasks failing in all five samples, so a retry would not have helped. The failures concentrated in values that need interpretation rather than copying: times written as words (dhai baje, half past two), number words, a refund amount, a cancellation reason and a city. 67 of the 73 failed calls (91.8%) were valid against the tool schema, and in an earlier pilot a validator-feedback loop never triggered while a quarter of calls carried wrong values. For clinic agents in code-mixed languages, schema validation is not a safety check; repeated-trial evaluation and read-back confirmation of interpreted values are needed. All prompts, replies and analysis code are public."
authors:
